.TH ENBAN 1 "2026-11-11" "enban" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
enban \- write, read, build and verify CD, DVD and Blu-ray discs
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B enban
.RB [ \-\-drive
.IR drive ]
.RB [ \-\-theme
.IR name ]
.br
.B enban info
.BI \-\-drive " drive"
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.br
.B enban write
.I image
.BI \-\-drive " drive"
.RB [ \-\-speed
.IR n ]
.RB [ \-\-no\-verify ]
.RB [ \-\-no\-eject ]
.br
.B enban read
.BI \-\-drive " drive"
.I image
.RB [ \-\-no\-verify ]
.br
.B enban verify
.BI \-\-drive " drive"
.RI [ image ]
.br
.B enban build
.I folder image
.RB [ \-\-fs
.IR list ]
.RB [ \-\-label
.IR name ]
.br
.B enban doctor
.br
.B enban version
.br
.B enban help
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B enban
opens a full-screen app on a terminal. It draws in the terminal's alternate
screen, reads keys itself and gives the screen back when it ends, on
.B q
or on an interrupt. Tab and the digits switch tabs, the arrows move between
the jobs on Home and Enter opens one, and
.B ?
shows the keys. The right pane describes the disc in the chosen drive.
On Write, Enter edits a field, space turns verify or eject on or off,
.B w
writes after the drive's name is typed, and
.B c
cancels a running write. On Read, Enter names the new image file, space
turns verify on or off,
.B r
reads the disc into it, and
.B c
cancels the read. On Verify, space turns the image comparison on or off,
Enter names the image,
.B v
verifies the disc, and
.B c
cancels the verify. Without a terminal the app never opens.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B info
Describe the drive and the disc in it: vendor, product and revision, the disc
profile, whether the disc is blank, appendable or closed, its sessions, the
free sectors and the write speeds the drive offers. On Linux it reads the drive
through
.BR xorriso .
Reading a drive on macOS is not supported yet, so info stops there.
.TP
.B write
Burn
.I image
to the blank disc in the drive. The image must be a whole number of 2048-byte
sectors and fit the free space. enban asks for the drive's name, as in
.BR sr0 ,
and writes only when it is typed. Then it reads the disc back, compares it
with the image and ejects the disc. It refuses a disc that is not blank.
.TP
.B read
Copy the readable sectors of the disc into
.IR image ,
read the disc again to compare, and print the SHA-256 of the copy. It refuses
a disc that holds no data and never overwrites a file.
.TP
.B verify
Read every sector of a disc that holds data and, when
.I image
is given, compare the disc with it. Prints how many sectors were readable,
how many differ and how many could not be read, and exits non-zero naming the
first sector that differs or cannot be read.
.TP
.B build
Make a new
.I image
from the files in
.IR folder ,
with ISO9660 always and Joliet and UDF unless
.B \-\-fs
leaves them out. macOS builds with hdiutil; Linux builds with xorriso, and
with genisoimage when UDF is asked for. The volume name is the folder's, or
.BR \-\-label ,
in the letters ISO9660 allows. It never overwrites a file.
.TP
.B doctor
List the tools enban needs and whether each is installed. Exits non-zero only
when a required tool is missing.
.TP
.B version
Print the version and the platform.
.TP
.B help
Print the usage page.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-drive " drive"
The drive info, write, read, verify and the app use, such as
.IR /dev/sr0 .
.TP
.B \-\-json
Print the result of info as one JSON object, for scripts.
.TP
.BI \-\-speed " n"
Write at
.I n
times the base speed of the disc, such as 8. By default the drive picks.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-verify
Write or read without the second pass that compares the disc and the image.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-eject
Leave the disc in the drive when the write ends.
.TP
.BI \-\-fs " list"
The file systems build writes, from iso9660, joliet and udf, separated by
commas; iso9660 is required. All three by default.
.TP
.BI \-\-label " name"
The volume name build writes, up to 32 characters. The folder's name by
default.
.TP
.BI \-\-theme " name"
Colours of the terminal interface:
.BR kodougu ,
.BR catppuccin\-mocha ,
.BR catppuccin\-latte ,
.BR dracula ,
.BR nord ,
.BR gruvbox ,
.B tokyo\-night
or
.BR mono .
The default is
.BR kodougu .
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B ENBAN_DRIVE
The drive when
.B \-\-drive
is not given.
.TP
.B ENBAN_THEME
The theme when
.B \-\-theme
is not given.
.TP
.B KODOUGU_THEME
The theme of every kodougu tool, when neither
.B \-\-theme
nor
.B ENBAN_THEME
is set.
.TP
.B KODOUGU_ACCENT
A colour such as #CBA6F7 that replaces the accent of the theme.
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
Turns colour off in every theme.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success, 1 on any failure or refusal.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR drutil (1),
.BR hdiutil (1),
.BR xorriso (1)
